latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the Grid Structure:\n   a. Identify key structures (gray frames, crosses, lines).\n   b. Segment the grid into regions based on these structures.\n   c. Detect border-like color structures within each region.\n\n2. Color Analysis:\n   a. For each region, count the number of squares of each color.\n   b. Calculate the ratio of colors within each region.\n   c. Identify dominant and minority colors.\n\n3. Identify Potential Cluster Areas:\n   a. Within each region, find existing color clusters.\n   b. Identify areas where clusters can potentially expand.\n\n4. Prioritize Changes:\n   a. Rank regions based on their potential for improvement.\n   b. Within each region, prioritize changes that would create the largest coherent clusters.\n\n5. Iterative Cluster Expansion:\n   a. Start with the highest priority region and color.\n   b. Expand clusters by changing adjacent squares, following these rules:\n      - Don't create 2x2 squares of the same color.\n      - Respect region boundaries.\n      - Preserve border-like structures.\n      - Maintain approximate color ratios.\n   c. After each change, recalculate priorities and ratios.\n\n6. Handle Small Isolated Areas:\n   a. Identify isolated color areas of 1-3 squares.\n   b. Merge these with adjacent larger clusters if it improves coherence.\n\n7. Boundary Refinement:\n   a. Smooth boundaries between color clusters.\n   b. Ensure refinements don't create 2x2 same-color squares.\n\n8. Asymmetry Check:\n   a. Evaluate if some regions have changed significantly more than others.\n   b. If asymmetry is too high, attempt to balance changes across regions.\n\n9. Minimal Change Verification:\n   a. Compare the current state to the initial state.\n   b. If changes are minimal and goals are met, consider stopping.\n\n10. Iteration Control:\n    a. Repeat steps 5-9 for a set number of iterations or until no further improvements can be made.\n    b. After each full iteration, evaluate overall improvement.\n\n11. Final Adjustment:\n    a. Make minor tweaks to ensure color ratios closely match the initial state.\n    b. Verify that all constraints are satisfied (no 2x2 squares, preserved structures).\n\n12. Output Validation:\n    a. Compare the final state to the initial state to ensure meaningful improvement.\n    b. Verify that the solution adheres to all identified patterns and rules.\n\nThis plan is designed to be adaptive to different grid configurations. It allows for varying degrees of change based on the initial state and potential for improvement. The iterative nature with constant re-evaluation ensures that we're making meaningful progress while respecting the unique characteristics of each grid.\n\nThe key is to balance multiple objectives: creating larger clusters, maintaining color ratios, respecting structural elements, and allowing for asymmetric changes when beneficial. By following this plan, we should be able to handle a wide variety of input grids and produce outputs that satisfy the observed patterns in all given examples."
